The laser book (route 'laser-study', layout sections/laser-study.html). 'chapters' is a list; each has an 'id', a 'title', an optional 'intro' and 'sections'. Each section has an 'id', a 'title', a 'text' (the easy version), an optional 'picture', a 'simpler' with only a 'text' (the same section told again for someone with no background at all, shown by the 'More simply' button: everyday words, no formula, no link, shorter than the section; leave 'simpler' out and the button goes too) and an optional 'deeper' with its own 'title' and 'text' (the full version, shown by the 'Dig deeper' button; leave 'deeper' out and the button goes too). The page writes the numbers (1, 1.1, 1.2 ...): never put numbers in a title, and the contents renumber themselves when chapters or sections move. PICTURES: each 'picture' has 'image' (a file name, e.g. 'images/theory/3.5.jpg': the book's pictures live in images/theory/, named by section number, with their sources in CREDITS.md there), 'alt' (what the picture shows, for screen readers), 'caption' (the line under it) and 'todo' (what the picture should show), and may have 'more': a list of further pictures, each { 'image', 'alt' }, stacked under the first with the one caption under them all (the laser classes' five labels). While 'image' is empty the page shows the 'todo' in a dashed box; put the file in 'image' and the box goes, the picture and its caption appear. TEXT: an empty line (

) starts a new paragraph, **two stars** make words bold, and every formula, symbol or number with a unit is LaTeX between dollar signs: $E = h
u$ inside a sentence, or $$ ... $$ as a paragraph of its own. In JSON every backslash of LaTeX is written twice ( rac). Keep the formulas of 'hy' and 'en' the same and in the same order; inside a formula the decimal point is a point in both languages. A formula must stay on one line and hold no ** or [..](..). Titles, pictures' words and the sources hold no formulas. A link to a chapter is [words](#chapter-pumping) and to a section [words](#section-ruby): # then chapter- or section- then its id; the link's words hold no numbers. A person, place, material or device a reader may not know is linked, at its first mention in a chapter, to Wikipedia: [Theodore Maiman](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_Maiman) in 'en'; in 'hy' to the Armenian article (https://hy.wikipedia.org/wiki/...) when there is one, else to the same English one. Write a space in the address as _ and brackets as %28 and %29 (a ')' would end the link); only en.wikipedia.org, hy.wikipedia.org and, where there is no article, a photo on commons.wikimedia.org are allowed. Such a link opens in a new tab. The Armenian is a first translation, to be read by a fluent speaker. RUSSIAN: every chapter's words also have 'ru' beside 'hy' and 'en' (titles, texts, 'simpler', 'deeper', the pictures' words; not 'head', 'contents', 'simulator' or 'sources'), shown by the RUS switch at the top right of each chapter and section: a section's switch shows that section in Russian, a chapter's the whole chapter; the site itself stays Armenian and English. The 'ru' text keeps the English one's formulas unchanged and in the same order, its links to chapters and sections, and links a name to the Russian Wikipedia article (https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/...) where there is one, else to the same English one.
